</SubSection>

<!-- lecture 18 -->

<SubSection title="Truss analysis: method of joints" id="joints">

Method of joints for truss analysis: 
<Enumerate>
	<Item>Draw a free-body diagram for the entire truss and solve for the external reaction forces and moments at the supports</Item>
	<Item>Draw a free-body diagram of a joint with at least 1 known force and at most two unknown forces</Item>
	<Item>Use the equations of equilibrium for the joint to solve for the unknown forces</Item>
	<Item>Repeat steps 2 and 3 to find the forces in the trusses you are interested in. </Item>
</Enumerate>

<p><em>Note:</em> Remember that truss members that are in compression "push back" on the pin joints, and truss members that are in tension "pull" on the pin joints. 
</p>

<!-- Suggestion: Make this diagram more interactive -->
<Image src='/sta/trusses/ExplodedTruss.jpg' width='6'></Image>

</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Truss analysis: method of sections" id="sections">

<p> 
The method of sections is helpful for determining the internal forces inside a truss member. The first step for the method of sections is the same as the firts step for the method of joints: Draw a FBD for the entire truss and solve for the external forces at the supports. 

Then, you "cut" the truss so that the internal force you want is "exposed" from the cut and analyze the two parts of the truss system to solve for the internal forces of the truss members. 
</p>

<Image src='/sta/trusses/MethodofSections.jpg' width='6'>Example of methods of sections for joint analysis</Image>

<!-- lecture 19  -->

</SubSection>

  <SubSection title = "Truss Determinacy and Stability" id = "truss_determinacy_and_stability">
    <p>
      Determinacy and stability are important concepts to understand when analyzing trusses.
      These concepts are related, but not the same.
    </p>
    <CalloutContainer slot = "cards">
      <CalloutCard title = "Review?">
        <p>
          Need a review of <strong>system classifications?</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          This content has also been explained <a href = "/sta/reaction_forces#system_classifications">earlier in the course.</a>
        </p>
      </CalloutCard>
    </CalloutContainer>
  </SubSection>

  <SubSubsection title = "Determinacy" id = "determinacy">
    <p>
      Determinacy is defined as whether or not a structure's internal forces can be determined using only the equations of equilibrium.
    </p>
  </SubSubsection>

  <SubSubsection title = "Stability" id = "stability">
    <p>
      Stability is defined as a truss's ability to maintain overall equilibrium under load.
      Stability is not the same as determinacy.
      Unstable structures should always be avoided.
      If a structure is unstable, do not bother to discuss determinacy.
    </p>
  </SubSubsection>

  <SubSubsection title = "Procedure" id = "procedure">
    <p>
      To determine the determinacy and stability of a truss, follow the folllowing procedure.
    </p>
    <DisplayTable id = "id" class = "mb-3">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class = "table-shaded"/>
          <th class = "table-shaded" style = "border-right: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color);">Determinacy</th>
          <th class = "table-shaded">Stability</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td style = "vertical-align: middle; border-right: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color);"><InlineEquation equation = "b + r < 2 j"/></td>
          <td style = "vertical-align: middle; border-right: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color);">(determinacy is only meaningful when a structure is stable)</td>
          <td style = "vertical-align: middle;">Unstable</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style = "border-right: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color);"><InlineEquation equation = "b + r = 2 j"/></td>
          <td style = "border-right: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color);">Determinate</td>
          <td style = "vertical-align: middle;" rowspan = "2" >Possibly stable, but depends on configuration</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style = "border-right: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color);"><InlineEquation equation = "b + r > 2 j"/></td>
          <td style = "border-right: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color);">Indeterminate</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </DisplayTable>
    <p>
      In the relation, <InlineEquation equation = "b"/> is the number of members, <InlineEquation equation = "r"/> is the number of reactions, and <InlineEquation equation = "j"/> is the number of joints.
      <InlineEquation equation = "b + r"/> is the number of unknowns and <InlineEquation equation = "2 j"/> is the number of equations.
      If the stability depends on the configuration, check for improper constraints and internal collapse mechanisms.
      The following are some examples of improper constraints.
    </p>
    <Row>
      <Col values = "12 lg-6">
        <Image src = "/sta/trusses/all_reactions_parallel.png" width = "5">Improper constraints, all reactions parallel</Image>
      </Col>
      <Col values = "12 lg-6">
        <Image src = "/sta/trusses/all_reactions_concurrent_at_one_point.png" width = "5">Improper constraints, all reactions concurrent at one point</Image>
      </Col>
    </Row>
    <p>
      If all reactions are parallel like in that of the first example, then the truss will be able to move in the <InlineEquation equation = "x"/>-direction.
      If all reaction forces are concurrent at one point (e.g., the left pin joint), no restoring moment can be generated about that point, which leads to instability.
    </p>
    <Image src = "/sta/trusses/square.png" width = "2.5">Internal collapse mechanism, square</Image>
    <p>
      Squares (and rectangles) are common examples of internal collapse mechanisms.
      The geometry (internal angles) of these shapes can change without the member lengths changing, causing instability.
    </p>
    <Example title = "Truss Determinacy and Stability 1" solution = "True" id = "truss_determinacy_and_stability_1">
      <Image src = "/sta/trusses/truss_determinacy_and_stability_1.png" width = "7"/>
      <p>
        Is the truss determinate?
      </p>
      <p>
        Is the truss stable?
      </p>
      <div slot = "solution">
        <Image src = "/sta/trusses/truss_determinacy_and_stability_1_solution.png" width = "7"/>
        <DisplayEquation equation = `\\begin{align} b &= 11 \\\\
          r &= 3 \\\\
          j &= 7 \\\\
          11 + 3 &= 2 \\cdot 7 \\end{align}`/>
        <p>
          The truss is determinate and stable due to no improper constraints or internal collapse mechanisms.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Example>
    <Example title = "Truss Determinacy and Stability 2" solution = "True" id = "truss_determinacy_and_stability_2">
      <Image src = "/sta/trusses/truss_determinacy_and_stability_2.png" width = "7"/>
      <p>
        Is the truss determinate?
      </p>
      <p>
        Is the truss stable?
      </p>
      <div slot = "solution">
        <Image src = "/sta/trusses/truss_determinacy_and_stability_2_solution.png" width = "7"/>
        <DisplayEquation equation = `\\begin{align} b &= 12 \\\\
          r &= 3 \\\\
          j &= 8 \\\\
          12 + 3 &< 2 \\cdot 8 \\end{align}`/>
        <p>
          The truss is unstable, internal collapse mechanism.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Example>
    <Example title = "Truss Determinacy and Stability 3" solution = "True" id = "truss_determinacy_and_stability_3">
      <Image src = "/sta/trusses/truss_determinacy_and_stability_3.png" width = "7"/>
      <p>
        Is the truss determinate?
      </p>
      <p>
        Is the truss stable?
      </p>
      <div slot = "solution">
        <Image src = "/sta/trusses/truss_determinacy_and_stability_3_solution.png" width = "7"/>
        <DisplayEquation equation = `\\begin{align} b &= 15 \\\\
          r &= 3 \\\\
          j &= 8 \\\\
          15 + 3 &> 2 \\cdot 8 \\end{align}`/>
        <p>
          The truss is indeterminate and stable due to no improper constraints or internal collapse mechanisms.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Example>
    <Example title = "Truss Determinacy and Stability 4" solution = "True" id = "truss_determinacy_and_stability_4">
      <Image src = "/sta/trusses/truss_determinacy_and_stability_4.png" width = "7"/>
      <p>
        Is the truss determinate?
      </p>
      <p>
        Is the truss stable?
      </p>
      <div slot = "solution">
        <Image src = "/sta/trusses/truss_determinacy_and_stability_4_solution.png" width = "7"/>
        <DisplayEquation equation = `\\begin{align} b &= 13 \\\\
          r &= 3 \\\\
          j &= 8 \\\\
          13 + 3 &= 2 \\cdot 8 \\end{align}`/>
        <p>
          The truss is determinate and unstable due to an internal collapse mechanism.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Example>
    <Example title = "Truss Determinacy and Stability 5" solution = "True" id = "truss_determinacy_and_stability_5">
      <Image src = "/sta/trusses/truss_determinacy_and_stability_5.png" width = "7"/>
      <p>
        Is the truss determinate?
      </p>
      <p>
        Is the truss stable?
      </p>
      <div slot = "solution">
        <Image src = "/sta/trusses/truss_determinacy_and_stability_5_solution.png" width = "7"/>
        <DisplayEquation equation = `\\begin{align} b &= 11 \\\\
          r &= 3 \\\\
          j &= 7 \\\\
          11 + 3 &= 2 \\cdot 7 \\end{align}`/>
        <p>
          The truss is determinate and unstable due to improper constraints, all reactions concurrent at one point.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Example>
  </SubSubsection>

</Layout>

<script src="/sta/trusses/canvases.js" is:inline></script>

<script is:inline>
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  // parse a class token like "data-option:aml-fr-c:a" -> { varName: "aml_fr_c", option: "a", token: "aml-fr-c" }
  function parseDataToken(token) {
    const parts = token.split(':');
    if (parts.length < 3) return null;
    const controllerToken = parts[1]; // "aml-fr-c"
    const option = parts.slice(2).join(':'); // "a" (handles extra colons if any)
    const varName = controllerToken.replace(/-/g, '_'); // "aml_fr_c"
    return { controllerToken, varName, option };
  }

  function updateOptionSpan(varName, option, controllerToken) {
    try {
      const controller = window[varName];
      const value = (controller && typeof controller.getOption === 'function') ? controller.getOption(option) : '';
      // class in DOM uses the dashed controller token, e.g. data-option:aml-fr-c:a
      const className = `data-option:${controllerToken}:${option}`;
      const els = document.getElementsByClassName(className);
      Array.from(els).forEach(el => {
        el.textContent = (value === null || value === undefined) ? '' : String(value);
      });
    } catch (err) {
      // non-fatal; helpful while debugging
      // console.warn('updateOptionSpan error', err);
    }
  }

  function updateAllOptionSpans() {
    // scan elements' classList for tokens that start with "data-option:"
    document.querySelectorAll('[class]').forEach(el => {
      Array.from(el.classList).forEach(cls => {
        if (!cls.startsWith('data-option:')) return;
        const info = parseDataToken(cls);
        if (!info) return;
        updateOptionSpan(info.varName, info.option, info.controllerToken);
      });
    });
  }

  // Wire inputs that have data-input:... classes so sliding updates the displayed value from the controller
  document.querySelectorAll('input').forEach(input => {
    Array.from(input.classList).forEach(cls => {
      if (!cls.startsWith('data-input:')) return;
      const parts = cls.split(':');
      if (parts.length < 3) return;
      const controllerToken = parts[1];
      const option = parts.slice(2).join(':');
      const varName = controllerToken.replace(/-/g, '_');
      const handler = () => updateOptionSpan(varName, option, controllerToken);
      input.addEventListener('input', handler);
      input.addEventListener('change', handler);
    });
  });

  // initial sync (delay a bit so canvases.js can initialize controllers)
  setTimeout(updateAllOptionSpans, 100);

  // keep UI in sync (optional; adjusts if controller updates from other sources)
  const syncHandle = setInterval(updateAllOptionSpans, 500);

  // expose for console debugging
  window.updateOptionSpans = updateAllOptionSpans;
});
</script>